    "q-52474": {
      "dom_id": "step-q52474",
      "name": "Question 7",
      "problem": "Which of the sequences below are geometric sequences?\n\nI. $-1,\\,-6,\\,-36,\\,…$\n\nII. $1,\\,-4,\\,-16,\\,…$\n\nIII. $2,\\,7,\\,12,\\,…$",
      "worked_solution": "A sequence is geometric if there is a common ratio between its terms. Let's check each sequence in turn.\n\n- Sequence I is geometric. For each pair of consecutive terms, the common ratio is the same:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}} & =\\frac{(-6)}{(-1)}=6 \\\\ \\frac{{a}_{3}}{{a}_{2}} & =\\frac{(-36)}{(-6)}=6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Sequence II is not geometric because not every pair of consecutive terms has the same ratio. To see this, we only need to go up to the third term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}} & =\\frac{(-4)}{1}=-4 \\\\ \\frac{{a}_{3}}{{a}_{2}} & =\\frac{(-16)}{(-4)}=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Sequence III is also not geometric because not every pair of consecutive terms has the same ratio. To see this, we only need to go up to the third term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}} & =\\frac{7}{2} \\\\ \\frac{{a}_{3}}{{a}_{2}} & =\\frac{12}{7}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn conclusion, only sequence I could be a geometric sequence.",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
