    "q-79509": {
      "dom_id": "step-q79509",
      "name": "Question 8",
      "problem": "Which of the sequences below are geometric sequences?\n\nI. $120,\\,80,\\,30,\\,…$\n\nII. $-243,\\,27,\\,-3,\\,…$\n\nIII. $225,\\,45,\\,9,\\,…$",
      "worked_solution": "A sequence is geometric if there is a common ratio between its terms. Let's check each sequence in turn.\n\n- Sequence I is not geometric because not every pair of consecutive terms has the same ratio. To see this, we only need to go up to the third term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}} & =\\frac{80}{120}=\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ \\frac{{a}_{3}}{{a}_{2}} & =\\frac{30}{80}=\\frac{3}{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Sequence II is geometric. For each pair of consecutive terms, the common ratio is the same:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}} & =\\frac{27}{(-243)}=-\\frac{1}{9} \\\\ \\frac{{a}_{3}}{{a}_{2}} & =\\frac{(-3)}{27}=-\\frac{1}{9}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Sequence III is also geometric. For each pair of consecutive terms, the common ratio is the same:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}} & =\\frac{45}{225}=\\frac{1}{5} \\\\ \\frac{{a}_{3}}{{a}_{2}} & =\\frac{9}{45}=\\frac{1}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn conclusion, only sequences II and III could be geometric sequences.",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
